Isocast for Debian and Ubuntu
=============================

H.264 (OpenH264)
----------------
Isocast's software H.264 encoder and live preview decoder use OpenH264, which this
package does not contain. Isocast checks an explicit absolute $ISOCAST_OPENH264
path first, then Cisco's verified per-user download, then the system loader's
libopenh264.so.8. Distro packages may depend on a working OpenH264 2.x >= 2.6
with that soname. Cisco's patent licence covers only its own downloaded binary;
system builds are provided by the distro. If no working codec is found, choose
Download at first launch or Not now to skip. For CLI-only use or to retry Cisco's
download, run as yourself (not root):

    isocast-openh264-install

It downloads Cisco's OpenH264 2.6.0 for your architecture from
https://ciscobinary.openh264.org over HTTPS, checks its SHA-256 and puts it
in ~/.local/lib/isocast. It is Cisco's binary, under Cisco's licence
(CISCO_H264_LICENSE.txt in this folder). OpenH264 Video Codec provided by
Cisco Systems, Inc.

`isocast-openh264-install --remove` removes only Cisco's library; a compatible
system library can still be used. Without either, Isocast still starts and a
supported NVIDIA encoder can still encode H.264. `isocast doctor` names the
codec in use or explains why it is unavailable.

Removing the package
--------------------
`apt remove isocast` and `apt purge isocast` leave every user's data alone:
recordings (~/Videos/Isocast), settings (~/.local/share/isocast) and the
OpenH264 each user installed (~/.local/lib/isocast; remove it with
`isocast-openh264-install --remove` first, or delete that folder).

Debian 12 (bookworm)
--------------------
Isocast's Linux capture needs PipeWire 1.0.5 or newer; bookworm has 0.3.65,
so the Debian 12 package depends on PipeWire from bookworm-backports.
Install that first, then the package:

    echo 'deb http://deb.debian.org/debian bookworm-backports main' |
      sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/backports.list
    sudo apt update
    sudo apt install -t bookworm-backports libpipewire-0.3-0 pipewire wireplumber
    sudo apt install ./isocast-*-debian12-$(dpkg --print-architecture).deb

Coming from the tarball
-----------------------
If you installed Isocast from the isocast-linux tarball before, remove that
copy first: its ~/.local/bin/isocast and isocast-ui come before /usr/bin on
your PATH. `~/.local/lib/isocast/install.sh --uninstall` removes it (and its
OpenH264; run isocast-openh264-install again afterwards). Recordings and
settings stay.
